  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nA number of factors came together to create a scorching afternoon in \nSan Diego County yesterday. First, the tropical moisture that has \nbeen funneling into the region for a few days effectively wiped out \nany cooling marine layer influence. Also, subsidence was likely \nsourced from multiple locations yesterday; the building high \npressure to the east and the compensating sinking air associated \nwith the shower activity over Orange County and areas further north. \n\nAs high pressure continues to build into Southern California, \ntropical clouds and showers will be pushed northward and \nanomalously hot conditions will expand to include the entire \nregion from today through Thursday. Additionally, southeasterly \nflow today and Wednesday will bring exceptionally high humidity to\nthe region, which will not only exacerbate the heat during the \nday, but will also limit the amount of cooling expected overnight.\nHigh and low temperatures running around 5 to 15 (locally 20) \ndegrees above normal along with high humidity will create major to\nextreme HeatRisk throughout the coastal basin today and \nWednesday. Similarly anomalous temperatures will create moderate \nHeatRisk in the mountains and deserts with areas of major HeatRisk\nin the low deserts. Note that this set up will be particularly \nanomalous and uncomfortable for coastal areas where the typical \nmarine layer influence will be weak or absent. It is advised to \nseek shelter in air-conditioned buildings and stay hydrated during\nthis heatwave.\n\nAs flow turns more southerly today, there will be sufficient \nmoisture for isolated to scattered monsoon showers and thunderstorms \nalong the mountain ridges from San Diego County northward up through \nthe Big Bear Lake area. The morning sounding at KNKX recorded PWATs \naround 1.8 inches, but modest CAPE values and capping will limit how \nmuch of that moisture can be realized as precipitation. Still, \nthunderstorms may locally produce rain rates over 0.5\" per hour \nalong with gusty winds, mainly in San Diego County. As high pressure \nmoves overhead on Wednesday, mountain thunderstorm chances will \nbecome quite limited (10-20%). Further capping on Thursday may \ninhibit any shower or storm development entirely.\n\nThe high pressure slowly begins to weaken from Thursday onward. \nModerate to major HeatRisk is expected to linger through Thursday \nacross the region and into Friday for the deserts. Thereafter, \ntemperatures continue to cool over the weekend, returning to near or \nslightly below normal from Sunday onward. Additionally, marine layer \nclouds will finally return beginning on Friday or Saturday. \nIncreasing onshore flow will allow the marine layer to deepen into\nearly next week.",
